1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,641
What is AI,
and what does it do in health care?

2
00:00:03,641 --> 00:00:08,210
Let's cover the core idea, how hospitals
use it, and four warning signs.

3
00:00:08,810 --> 00:00:13,594
Most AI isn't programmed rule by rule.
It learns patterns from examples.

4
00:00:13,594 --> 00:00:17,984
A spam filter studies thousands
of emails marked spam or not spam,

5
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:20,940
then makes a guess about each new one.

6
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:24,303
AI tools mostly do one of two jobs.

7
00:00:24,303 --> 00:00:27,297
Predictive AI sorts, scores, or flags.

8
00:00:27,297 --> 00:00:31,595
Generative AI creates new content,
like text or images.

9
00:00:31,595 --> 00:00:36,370
Chat tools such as ChatGPT
and Gemini are generative AI.

10
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:40,222
Prediction tools are
already common in hospitals.

11
00:00:40,222 --> 00:00:43,539
In 2023, about two in three U.S.
hospitals used AI

12
00:00:43,539 --> 00:00:47,572
or other prediction tools in
their electronic health records,

13
00:00:47,572 --> 00:00:52,980
most often to flag patients who might
get worse, or who need follow-up care.

14
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:55,876
Some tools read medical images.

15
00:00:55,876 --> 00:01:00,469
In 2018, the FDA authorized an AI
system that checks eye photos

16
00:01:00,469 --> 00:01:04,632
for diabetes-related eye
disease in primary care offices,

17
00:01:04,632 --> 00:01:08,620
with no eye specialist
needed to read the photos.

18
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:10,959
Generative AI is newer.

19
00:01:10,959 --> 00:01:17,118
It can draft clinical notes, or replies to
patient messages, for a person to review.

20
00:01:17,118 --> 00:01:22,010
In 2024, about one in three U.S.
hospitals reported using it.

21
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:27,723
Now, four warning signs. One: the data it
learned from may not fit your patients.

22
00:01:27,723 --> 00:01:31,901
A tool built in one kind of hospital
can lose accuracy in another,

23
00:01:31,901 --> 00:01:35,170
with different people,
equipment, or records.

24
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:40,241
Two: it can aim at the wrong target.
A widely used tool picked patients

25
00:01:40,241 --> 00:01:43,222
for extra care by predicting costs,
not health.

26
00:01:43,222 --> 00:01:48,127
Less was spent on equally sick Black
patients, so it passed over many of them.

27
00:01:48,127 --> 00:01:50,984
Researchers estimated
that without this bias,

28
00:01:50,984 --> 00:01:54,399
Black patients' share of
those picked would have risen

29
00:01:54,399 --> 00:01:56,600
from about 18 to 47 percent.

30
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:00,687
Three: it can make things up,
and still sound sure.

31
00:02:00,687 --> 00:02:03,839
In a 2023 study,
more than half the references

32
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:06,790
from an early version
of ChatGPT were fake.

33
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:10,610
A newer version still
invented almost one in five.

34
00:02:11,210 --> 00:02:13,404
Four: people tend to over-trust it.

35
00:02:13,404 --> 00:02:17,608
In a review of studies, wrong advice
from a computer tool made wrong

36
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:21,569
decisions about 26 percent more
likely than with no tool at all.

37
00:02:21,569 --> 00:02:25,260
So a trained person must check it,
and make the call.

38
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:30,018
So: AI learns patterns from examples,
then predicts or creates.

39
00:02:30,018 --> 00:02:32,097
It's useful, and it has limits.

40
00:02:32,097 --> 00:02:37,370
Next: what makes rural health care
different, and why that matters for AI.
